  "question": {
    "text": "One end of a long metallic wire of length&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> is tied to the ceiling. The other end is tied to a massless spring of spring constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math>. A mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> hangs freely from the free end of the spring. The area of cross-section and Young’s modulus of the wire are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math> respectively. If the mass is slightly pulled down and released, it will oscillate with a time period&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> equal to&nbsp;",
    "image": null
  },
